# Fresh working-draft warning with scope and state.
setp(0,'WORKING REVISION — NOT FOR SUBMISSION. This manuscript has been updated after correction of a nitrene atom-index error in pose-feature generation. Corrected representation, acquisition, independent progression, and 13-pair cross-catalyst results are included. Some upstream-dependent analyses and SI material still require reconciliation; do not submit until every retained result and the data package have been traced to the corrected feature version.')
setp(6,'Substrate-scope construction is an information-acquisition problem: the order in which substrates are examined determines how efficiently catalyst behaviour is learned. Here, catalyst-contextual representation and information-guided substrate ordering were retrospectively reevaluated in two small iron-porphyrin datasets for enantioselective intramolecular C(sp3)–H amination after correction of a nitrene atom-mapping error. In Fe(P7)Cl Elastic Net comparisons, adding pose descriptors reduced target-held-out MAE from 12.61 to 7.98 ee points and family-held-out MAE from 10.41 to 8.54; the family result remains heterogeneous across 17 small, imbalanced groups. In corrected common-test acquisition analyses, uncertainty–diversity reduced mean ee MAE relative to source order by 6.99 points for Fe(P7)Cl and 2.50 for the Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl cohort, but its advantage over random selection was smaller and varied across splits. Query by committee had lower mean ee error in both common-test datasets. Corrected yield comparisons differed between catalysts, and full-pose/acquisition combinations did not outperform structure-only baselines on average. All results are retrospective and conditional on the two published datasets; additional trajectory and SI analyses remain under correction.')
# Corrected methods and error disclosure.
setp(36,d.paragraphs[36].text+' During revision, direct coordinate checks found that the original pipeline sometimes treated molecular atom index zero as the nitrene nitrogen, although that index can identify a carbon for suffix-style nitrene SMILES. The corrected pipeline obtains the nitrene index from the mapped pose molecule and uses it consistently for nitrene-anchored channels and transferable-hydrogen selection. Original coordinates and outcomes were preserved; affected pose features and downstream analyses were regenerated. A source-to-output correction inventory is provided with the computational data.')
# Better explain preprocessing screen.
setp(39,'A substrate, rather than an individual pose, is the statistical observation; all its poses remain in the same split. The original 38-substrate representation benchmark applied a label-blind global constant-feature screen before splitting, while standardisation and model fitting were performed within training data. Corrected Elastic Net held-out predictions were reproduced with training-only constant-feature screening when retaining the already selected hyperparameters; this does not verify model selection or other estimators. All feature-generation and preprocessing details for the corrected runs are reported in the SI.')
# Replace outdated representation results.
setp(63,'In the corrected matched 38-substrate Fe(P7)Cl Elastic Net comparison (Fig. 3), pose descriptors reduced target-held-out MAE from 12.612 to 7.979 ee points (36.7%). In family-held-out validation, MAE decreased from 10.406 to 8.538 ee points (17.9%). The two validation protocols answer different questions and the fold-level estimates remain conditional on one small dataset.')
setp(65,'Figure 3. Corrected Fe(P7)Cl Elastic Net comparisons using the same 38 substrates and held-out memberships. Bars show MAE and error bars show the reported conditional 95% bootstrap intervals for each model; they are not a test of the difference between models. Target- and family-held-out validation address distinct prediction settings.')
setp(66,'The corrected family-held-out difference is 1.87 ee points. It does not establish reliable prediction for new substrate families. The target-held-out reduction is 36.7%, while the family-held-out reduction is 17.9%; their difference is descriptive because the split schemes and fitted models differ. Neither result is independent prospective validation.')
setp(67,'A post hoc breakdown of the corrected predictions shows lower pose MAE in 11 of 17 families and lower absolute error for 23 of 38 substrates. The largest family still favours structure-only (MAE 5.25 versus 6.23 ee points). Equal weighting of family MAEs gives 11.99 versus 8.72, but nine families contain one substrate and this sensitivity summary is not independent validation (SI). Prediction for new catalysts or reaction classes remains untested.')
setp(68,'For the four Fe(P7)Cl examples at or below 50% ee, the corrected structure-plus-pose Elastic Net overpredicted each held-out value; mean prediction-minus-observation was +17.85 ee points in target holdout and +18.56 in family holdout. This small post hoc subset describes a limitation rather than general low-ee performance (SI).')
setp(69,'The corrected Stage 2pp ablation does not support the earlier numerical comparison. For the fixed P7 target-held-out Elastic Net analysis, MAE changed from 8.270 for Stage 2p to 10.464 after adding occupancy/flexibility features. The corrected run uses the regenerated Stage 2pp geometry and is retained in the project folder; full interpretation of its family and cross-catalyst comparisons remains subject to SI reconciliation.')
setp(70,'The Stage 1-v2 pose selection and dependent Stage 2-v2/Stage 3a seed-pilot models were regenerated from corrected pose interactions. The selector retained 600 poses across 12 substrate sets; 148 pose identities changed across four sets. On the 11 ee-labelled records available for modeling, MAE was 6.78 for the Stage 2 seed baseline, 15.20 for the Stage 2-v2 selected-pose model, 6.89 for graph-only Stage 3a-alt, and 13.20 for the Stage 3a reference-shell model. This small retrospective pilot does not support an accuracy gain from selected-pose filtering or the reference-shell features; it does not establish that these representations are generally inferior.')
# Mean-prefix route replay, corrected and clearly described.
setp(81,'The corrected dedicated Ridge mean-prefix ee MAEs for source order and uncertainty–diversity are 25.508 and 7.475 for Fe(P7)Cl and 16.977 and 9.641 for the Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl cohort. These correspond to descriptive reductions of 70.7% and 43.2%, respectively, relative to the archived source-order routes. The rerun preserves the route-specific initial pairs; it therefore combines starting-scope and subsequent ordering effects. Random continuation was rerun with three continuations, rather than the archived 500 draws, so it is not a direct replacement for the historical random summary.')
setp(83,'Across 50 shared starting pairs, uncertainty–diversity had lower remaining-pool mean-prefix MAE than source-order continuation for 50/50 Fe(P7)Cl pairs and 45/50 Fe(CMC-Por)Cl pairs. Mean paired source-order-minus-uncertainty–diversity differences were 9.83 points (conditional pair-bootstrap interval 8.61 to 11.04) and 4.84 points (3.96 to 5.69). Against the mean of three random continuations, uncertainty–diversity was lower for 39/50 and 46/50 pairs. These intervals describe sampled starts in the fixed datasets and not uncertainty over new substrates or catalysts.')
setp(84,'On the seven-record locked Fe(CMC-Por)Cl audit, the corrected mean paired source-order-minus-uncertainty–diversity difference was 3.88 ee points (conditional bootstrap interval 2.69 to 5.05), with uncertainty–diversity lower for 43/50 starting pairs. This small fixed audit remains conditional on its seven records and does not establish performance on a new catalyst.')
setp(85,'In the corrected common-test analysis, mean paired source-order-minus-uncertainty–diversity MAE differences across 20 overlapping splits were 6.99 ee points for Fe(P7)Cl and 2.50 for Fe(CMC-Por)Cl; 19/20 and 11/20 splits favoured uncertainty–diversity. Against random continuation, mean differences were 1.45 and 1.22 points, and 15/20 and 12/20 splits favoured uncertainty–diversity. These are repeated splits of the same datasets, not independent studies or population-level significance tests.')
setp(86,'In the corrected established-method comparison, mean common-test ee MAE for uncertainty–diversity / query by committee / expected improvement / Thompson sampling was 13.72 / 13.08 / 13.90 / 14.42 for Fe(P7)Cl and 13.83 / 13.40 / 16.41 / 15.33 for Fe(CMC-Por)Cl aryl substrates. Query by committee had lower mean error in both datasets. These overlapping retrospective splits compare specific implementations and do not establish a universal method ranking; expected improvement and Thompson sampling also optimise a different objective.')
setp(87,'In the corrected 21-setting weight grid, the default ranked 8th of 21 for Fe(P7)Cl and 11th of 21 for Fe(CMC-Por)Cl by mean split MAE. Mean split MAE ranged from 13.27 to 15.40 for Fe(P7)Cl and from 13.45 to 16.33 for Fe(CMC-Por)Cl. These descriptive grid ranks do not identify an optimum or a generally preferred weighting; split overlap and the finite grid limit the inference.')
setp(88,'Across the corrected grid, default-weight comparisons varied by dataset and comparator. These descriptive averages over overlapping splits are reported in the correction tables; they do not demonstrate that the default weighting is uniformly superior or transferable.')
setp(89,'In the corrected matched factorial analysis, mean held-out MAE for structure/source, structure/acquisition, full-pose/source and full-pose/acquisition was 11.34, 12.07, 20.71 and 13.72 ee points for Fe(P7)Cl, and 9.62, 10.77, 16.33 and 13.83 for Fe(CMC-Por)Cl. Source-order-minus-acquisition differences were −0.73 and −1.15 for structure-only and 6.99 and 2.50 for full-pose models. This indicates a representation-dependent ordering effect in these corrected retrospective splits, but the combined pose/acquisition cell did not outperform structure-only/source in either dataset.')
setp(90,'In the corrected mixed-pool Fe(CMC-Por)Cl analysis, uncertainty–diversity MAE was 13.27 for held-out aryl records and 18.15 for sulfonyl records when the candidate pool included both classes; with an aryl-only candidate pool, the corresponding values were 13.27 and 33.36. In a separate corrected sulfonyl-only replay, source order, uncertainty–diversity and random gave 17.98, 19.22 and 19.63 ee points. These retrospective comparisons vary candidate and test composition and are not a causal test that adding sulfonyl candidates improves generalisation.')
